---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AnalyseTextarea from '../components/AnalyseTextarea.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Devil's Advocate — for readers | The Whetstone"
  description="Highlight any argument online and see its structure, its assumptions, and the strongest case against it. Free Chrome extension — coming to the Web Store."
  canonicalPath="/reader"
>
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-16">

    <nav class="mb-10">
      <a href="/" class="text-xs text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors">← The Whetstone</a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="mb-10">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-indigo-500 uppercase mb-3">For readers</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        Devil's Advocate
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-500 leading-relaxed max-w-xl">
        Highlight any argument online and see its structure, its assumptions, and the strongest case against it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="mb-14">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3 bg-indigo-600 text-white text-sm font-medium rounded-lg cursor-default select-none opacity-90">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke-width="2"/>
          <polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        </svg>
        Coming to the Chrome Web Store
      </span>
      <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
        Free, ad-supported. Premium ~£5/mo when launched.
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-indigo-500 hover:text-indigo-700 underline underline-offset-2 ml-1">How it works →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Live demo -->
    <AnalyseTextarea />

    <!-- Three modes -->
    <section class="mt-16 mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-gray-400 uppercase mb-8">Three reading modes</h2>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Casual</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            For news, Substack, social media, and most long-form web reading. Short, non-judgmental output. The default.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Research</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            For academic papers, preprints, and reports. Full Toulmin diagram, deeper citation chains, BibTeX export. Premium.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Review</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            For evaluating someone else's draft or submission. Rubric-style output you can paste into feedback. Premium.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-xs text-gray-400">Casual mode is live in the demo above. Research and Review are in development.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="mb-14">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-gray-400 uppercase mb-8">Questions</h2>
      <dl class="space-y-8">

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">What does this actually do?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            It reads the text you give it as an argument — not as prose, not as opinion. It surfaces what's
            being claimed, what evidence is offered, and the usually-unstated assumption connecting them
            (the warrant). Then it writes the strongest single-paragraph case for the opposing position.
            The result is a structural view of the argument, not a verdict on who is right.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">What doesn't it do?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            It doesn't adjudicate whether specific claims are accurate. It doesn't label content as
            good or bad. It doesn't tell you what to think or what to share. It analyses structure —
            how the argument is built — not whether the underlying assertions are true. That remains
            the reader's job.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why "the whetstone"?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            A whetstone is what you sharpen a blade against. The tool functions the same way for
            thinking: it gives your reasoning something to push against. You leave the encounter
            with a clearer sense of where the argument is strong and where it needs more work — and
            with the strongest possible counter-position in hand.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">How is my data handled?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            The extension sends text to Google's Gemini API directly from your browser using an API
            key you supply. Nothing passes through any server operated by the developer.
            The web demo (this page) sends text to a Cloudflare Pages Function which forwards it to
            Gemini. Neither Cloudflare nor the developer stores the text.
            Full details: <a href="/privacy" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-800 underline underline-offset-2">privacy policy</a>.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Why open-source?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            The prompts that drive the analysis and the Toulmin schema the tool uses are visible and
            auditable in the public repository. A tool that shapes how people read arguments should
            be transparent about how it works.
            <a href="https://github.com/Daniell-44/The_Whetstone_Project" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-800 underline underline-offset-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View on GitHub →</a>
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">When does Creator Studio ship?</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            The same analysis engine, pointed at your own writing, is in development. No timeline
            commitment here. The extension ships first, the engine gets validated on real reading
            sessions, then the studio follows.
            <a href="/creator" class="text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-800 underline underline-offset-2">See what's planned →</a>
          </dd>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </section>

  </main>
</Base>
